we've just watched a film on the high  street it's clearly a really hard time  for so many businesses and the ippr  estimates that more than half a million  could go under this spring without more  government support what's your analysis  that's absolutely right and i think the  wider point is that uh we are still in  the crisis  obviously there's a roadmap out of it  but you keep asking the question  should we be moving to solve the public  finances now or should we be dealing  with a crisis  and i'm absolutely clear on this we have  got to sustain the support for  businesses  until we get to the other side of this  and perhaps the thing to reflect on  is all of the things that the chancellor  has already done  will almost be in vain if lots of  businesses fail in the next three months  so i'm categorically in that camp and of  course that does mean  the high street businesses yes welcome  the money that's been talked about today  but we've got to think about some of the  underlying points as well and of course  that includes business rates reform  and just to unpick some of what you said  there you're talking about the support  needed to continue  otherwise businesses will fail what kind  of time frame are you  um talking about i thought it was pretty  clear from rishi sunak that  you know they want to extend the support  to the end of the road map which is in  june  but do you think that actually some of  the support or needs to actually go a  bit further  well it's interesting because business  is not sort of uniform is it here  so quite rightly there's a lot of talk  about the businesses that have been most  seriously affected that's what your film  just talked about and i hope that for  them it really is a question  of getting to the reopening perhaps a  little bit on that because anyone who's  running a business knows that the period  in which you're  gearing back up is also very vulnerable  in cash flow  so but i'm optimistic that by the summer  we will see that consumer-led  recovery but there are other businesses  where the  other sectors where the damage is much  much lower long-term let's go to the  other extreme perhaps an  aerospace business lots of those in the  west midlands they are not  suddenly going to bounce back they've  still got lots of people on furlough  their levels of demand are very low and  the way they're going to need to come  back  is by investing in new kittens for the  upturn  and that's all about the sort of tax  advantages for them  and that is a much much longer burn that  some of those really competitive sectors  will be able to come back strongly it's  interesting because you're touching  there on sort of long-term  tax reform business rates for example to  help the high street things  directed at certain industries to help  their  working climate be a bit more attractive  what kind of things do you want to see  well i think the first thing around tax  that we're already seeing actually is  the tax on  jobs or the encouragement of businesses  to take more people on  the primary challenge i'll be honest  about the situation in the west midlands  85 000 more people claiming uh universal  credit benefits  than this time last year that is an  enormous change  so the headroom that the chancellor may  still have left  have already i think been applied quite  wisely to some of the business  advantages taking on apprentices so that  3 thousand pound allowance there  the kickstart scheme obviously and then  of course what's  also uh being done hopefully around  there's a talk in the papers today about  national insurance holidays for  recruiting  so that would all try to make it more  attractive for businesses  to recruit people but then we're also  going to need to think about what helps  companies invest in the long term and  the old  the best approach there is definitely  tax allowance against investment  what about corporation tax a lot of  discussion around uh potential rises to  corporation tax do you think that would  be  a good idea to try and get the public  finances in order  well let's take the question a little  bit more broadly then we'll come back to  corporation tax head on  i mean obviously we have got to over  time  address the public finances anyone who  denies that  i'm afraid is denying the reality but as  your first question juror  it's a question of when and it's also  true to say  that we say we've all been in this  together but actually some  sectors have had better times and some  individuals have had better times  so it is going to be necessary to see  some tax rises in the areas that have  probably done better  so you know the talk about online online  taxes i i do again say categorically  that is the right thing to do  they have had a good time it is right  they bear a little bit more of the  burden  on corporation tax i would regret it  personally because i do think britain's  very competitive corporation tax has  been right  but i would fully accept there are some  really tough choices for the chancellor  and if the end judgment is that's going  to go up a few points as he's talked  about in the papers today we'd still be  relatively competitive  and i would accept that possibly as a  price to pay  um you mentioned online tax and it's  it's a fiendishly difficult  place to tax because of the  international  uh global state of affairs and what kind  of things  could the chancellor look at i mean  there's for example on the sunday  telegraph today  reports of new tax on online deliveries  is that the kind of thing that you think  would work  you could do i mean you could you can  have an online sales tax it could be  really very blunt like that and of  course  the delivery tax has been talked about  but also actually informal business  rates would  if you just change the balance of  contribution between  high street premises and say  distribution warehouses you can tilt to  different sectors of the economy so  there are ways of doing it  and you're of course talking to us from  birmingham and mayor of the west  midlands and how concerned are you  about the pandemic's impact on the  leveling up  agenda it's really interesting question  actually  because what we have seen is that  pre-pandemic this place the economy was  performing  really well we had the fastest growth of  any region outside london over the  previous 10 years actually record  employment numbers great investment  but very sadly it looks as though we may  be the worst affected region so from  best to worse  is pretty uh dramatic so there is an  effect  but what will ultimately drive levelling  up is  all of the work around retraining  opportunity skills  and we can continue to do that and  actually in the region we continued  to try to make great progress on that so  if i was asking for one thing  around leveling up it wouldn't  necessarily about be about  roads and bridges and all of that it  actually let's really concentrate  on the retraining of the people who've  lost their job and get them ready for  the industries of the future  and in our case the whole electric  vehicle industry is critical  and of course the digital sector and  just one step i think your viewers found  interesting so free  all the retraining we're doing in  digital anyone who goes through our  schemes  almost anyone is employed after them  there are still parts of the economy  that are growing and that's where the  retraining needs to be focused  are you worried that the government's  taken its eye off the leveling up agenda  that it talked about so much during the  election  uh no i'm not actually uh i am i would  be gosh  uh almost superhuman not to have uh had  to move  complete focus to tackling the pandemic  of course and so perhaps some of the  things that we would otherwise have seen  haven't uh come through i i would accept  that but actually is there an  utter commitment to it we've heard about  the leveling up fund for example  the commitment of infrastructure spend  in the regions is absolutely there i  hope we're going to hear about the towns  fund  that's a good practical frontline piece  around leveling up and as i say  a lot of money is being committed on  this retraining and that will in the  long term  be the best way of raising opportunity  in places like this  i asked richard cena about the roadmap  out of lockdown as well he made it very  clear that the dates given are the  earliest dates  at which things can be eased  but at the same time if it's data driven  should they also be prepared to speed  things up  if the data is better than expected we  know of course they're willing to slow  things down if things  aren't going in the way that they want  so  um let me try to explain why i think  what's been said is logical and um  i haven't sort of had this described to  me by those in central government but my  own working out of it is  at each stage we have got to assess the  progress  we know that frankly when we unlocked  after the second lockdown  the virus banks back up what wrote the  time delays in the roadmap  are to see that we have not got an  increase  from each stage of it and then if you so  if you look at the three weeks then the  five weeks to me  that is logical because if you act  on the next thing before you know with  the lead time around the spread of the  virus  what's happened with the previous stage  you may be making the wrong  judgment i personally i asked for a  cautious approach to this  in the west midlands we still have a  pretty stubborn level of infections  about 169 per hundred thousand  so for us and the overwhelming reaction  from business there this week has been  yes understand why that cautious but  steady approach is there  because it allows us to assess progress  as i've described so we will never have  to go back  at the same time though lockdown of  course has consequences too we just  heard from paul johnson of the  institute for fiscal um studies who said  you know every week of additional  lockdown is costing not just the economy  but it's also costing so many people  so much in terms of their mental health  their education their work and  everything else  it should be moved as quickly as  possible consistent with doing it safely  if we can do it quicker we absolutely  should  you disagree then no if we if we  absolutely can and all the evidence is  pointing at that  of course i'm sure no one would wish to  say no to that  what i tried to do was explain why i  think it is utterly  logical that the time delays in the plan  are  as they are i would definitely like it  to go faster goodness  i lead the region that's had a terrible  effect in every sense  but i do think that it is far more  important that we do this  once and for all and get it right and  never risk the bounce back  that we saw actually twice in the second  half of last year

